{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat reader-brief-claims.md packet-claims-1.json'","aggregated_output":"# Gate 2 claims reading (read-only)\n\nYou are an independent reader. Read only this brief and `packet-claims.json`. Do not open other files or run commands or model calls.\n\nEach entry is one answer of a research step to one article. It lists the **claims** the answer must keep, **stored reference items**\n(how an earlier answer kept them), the **link rule**, the **open theses** the answer could link to, the answer's **new theses**, and its\n**kept evidence** items with the thesis ids each links to.\n\nFor each claim, decide whether the answer keeps it: a kept evidence item states the same figure or event for the same measure as the\nclaim (the item may carry more, and the facts may sit in one item). A detail of the claim (a speaker, a count of companies, a\npercentage) is required only where the stored reference items state it. Then decide whether it is linked as the link rule says.\nJudge by meaning, not by matching words. If `answer_usable` is false, every claim is not kept.\n\nReturn one JSON object per entry, one per line, in packet order, and nothing else:\n`{\"n\": 1, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"kept\": true|false, \"linked_as_required\": true|false, \"item\": \"<evidence id or null>\", \"why\": \"one sentence\"}]}`\n[\n {\n  \"n\": 1,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K1\",\n    \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K2\",\n    \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the cabinet designed to house 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, packing 144 GPUs per unit — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane that connects the system's modules remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint. The design had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027. Nvidia rejected the report (see separate item).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed, stating that its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-kyber\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its Vera Rubin Ultra chips — reach first shipments by the end of calendar 2028, following the reported delay of more than 12 months from its planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Kyber NVL144 (and the larger NVL576 multi-rack system) begin first shipments — the step that starts revenue for NVIDIA's next-generation Rubin Ultra platform — by the end of calendar 2028, as the reported delayed schedule implies.\",\n    \"period\": \"End of calendar 2028 (the delayed debut date SemiAnalysis reports, versus the original 2027 launch with Vera Rubin Ultra); set by the product event date the item supports.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-kyber-delay\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, citing manufacturing difficulty with the specialised multi-layer PCB midplane that connects electronic modules in the system.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-nvl576\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis also said NVL576 — a larger NVIDIA system linking eight racks via optical connections — is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-backup-plan-scrapped\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar power/scale — was scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers rejected the design as awkward and operationally burdensome, leaving NVIDIA with no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra; the firm predicted that could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-nvidia-denial\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected the SemiAnalysis report on the Kyber delay and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-rubin-shipping\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-dc-revenue-estimate\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 2,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K1\",\n    \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K2\",\n    \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the cabinet designed to house 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, packing 144 GPUs per unit — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane that connects the system's modules remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint. The design had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027. Nvidia rejected the report (see separate item).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed, stating that its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-1\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the system that debuts with Vera Rubin Ultra and carries the 2027 high-end AI rack cycle — begin shipping (the step that brings revenue) in calendar 2027, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis projects?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Kyber NVL144 / Vera Rubin Ultra rack systems reach first shipments in calendar 2027 or are pushed to 2028 (with the larger NVL576 either delayed or limited to small volumes).\",\n    \"period\": \"End of calendar 2027: the year the item says Kyber had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra before the reported more-than-12-month delay to 2028, so first shipments by then can be checked.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis said in a post that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture — the rack-scale system slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027 — has been delayed to 2028 because its specialized multi-layer PCB midplane remains challenging to manufacture, and that the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\",\n     \"The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed to 2028, stating \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, CNBC reported.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA's backup plan of bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar scale-up performance was scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers rejected the design as odd and operationally burdensome, leaving NVIDIA with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra\\\" — which the firm said could give Advanced Micro Devices and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 3,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The four largest AI hyperscalers plan to spend roughly $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the fool.com analysis, which describes this planned spend as the baseline for a sharp increase it expects next year (published 2026-07-07).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), up from around $650 billion planned for 2026, as reported by fool.com (published 2026-07-07).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers (Amazon, Google, Microsoft, Meta) spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027, and does their combined spend approach the >$1 trillion NVIDIA projects for the group?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2027 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against NVIDIA's claimed >$1 trillion for 2027.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar year 2027; settled by the hyperscalers' Q4 2027 earnings and capex disclosures (reported early 2028), the most direct public releases for full-year 2027 spend.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to fool.com (The Motley Fool), the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and NVIDIA claims the group will spend more than $1 trillion in 2027. These are a plan and a projection, not realized spending figures.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\",\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 4,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The four largest AI hyperscalers plan to spend roughly $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the fool.com analysis, which describes this planned spend as the baseline for a sharp increase it expects next year (published 2026-07-07).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), up from around $650 billion planned for 2026, as reported by fool.com (published 2026-07-07).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"fool.com reports that in the current year (calendar 2026) the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia claims that next year (calendar 2027) the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures, up from roughly $650 billion planned for this year, according to fool.com.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 5,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K1\",\n    \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K2\",\n    \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"NH1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"NH1\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-kyber-2027\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Rubin Ultra rack-scale generation — the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture and the larger NVL576 system — begin shipping in calendar 2027 as planned, rather than slipping more than 12 months to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack-scale systems confirmed in calendar 2027, versus a slip to 2028 or later.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 — set by the item: Kyber had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, and the disputed SemiAnalysis report pushes it by more than 12 months to 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture — the server cabinet designed to house 144 GPUs and slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer printed circuit board (PCB) midplane at the heart of the system remains difficult to manufacture.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-2027\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed to 2028, stating \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-2027\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia's NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely to be delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-2027\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to deliver comparable scale-up capacity — has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its awkward design and heavy operational burden, leaving Nvidia with, in the firm's words, \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra\\\"; SemiAnalysis predicted this could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate.\",\n     \"\\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-2027\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"The report states that Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping this fall (fall 2026) to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projected that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 6,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"G\",\n    \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia's revenue grew about 85%, with a GAAP gross margin of 74.9% and an operating margin of roughly 66%, according to the column's comparison of Nvidia with Micron's latest quarter; the period and quarter for Nvidia's figures are not specified in the item.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, a figure that includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other AI plumbing.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Memory supplier Micron reported fiscal Q3 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion, up from $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, GAAP gross margin of 84.6% (up from 37.7%) and operating margin of 80.4% (up from 23.3%); the column says rising memory-company margins mean higher costs for device makers, server makers and cloud providers.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent.\",\n     \"When Micron’s profit margins grow, it means higher costs for others. If memory companies earn more, then device makers, server makers, cloud providers, and eventually consumers could end up paying more for electronics.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 7,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion — the level Nvidia is said to project.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"fool.com's Keithen Drury wrote that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he said would drive even higher demand for memory chips and for Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron announced that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028; the article adds that new industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory supply for at least the next year and a half and potentially driving memory chip prices even higher as AI data-center demand persists.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\",\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 8,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\",\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"mem-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will memory (DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory) contract prices rise over calendar 2026–2027, pushing up the memory cost embedded in AI infrastructure and devices that NVIDIA and its customers depend on?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The change in DRAM/NAND/HBM contract prices and the resulting memory-content cost per AI rack over calendar 2026–2027.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2026–2027 (the item's 'second half of 2026 and beyond').\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research, reported by MarketScale, estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\",\n     \"The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; as a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5–10% on the GB200 to 25–30% on the VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\",\n     \"mem-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's Vera Rubin first shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, according to the item.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The item reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\",\n     \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"mem-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the standard estimate, a reminder that procurement structure materially moves the number.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\",\n     \"mem-1\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 9,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K1\",\n    \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K2\",\n    \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"NH1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"NH1\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-rubin-ultra-kyber-2027\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack designed to house its Rubin Ultra chips and carry its high-end data-center value — begin shipping by end of calendar 2027 as originally slated, or slip to 2028 (or later) as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation of first Kyber NVL144 / Vera Rubin Ultra rack shipments by end of calendar 2027, versus the reported more-than-12-month delay to 2028.\",\n    \"period\": \"First shipments by end of calendar 2027 — the debut date the item gives ('had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra ... in 2027'); the reported slip is to 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1-kyber-delay\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture — the NVL144 cabinet that packs 144 of Nvidia's most powerful chips and had been slated to debut with the Vera Rubin Ultra system in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis added that NVL576, a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-rubin-ultra-kyber-2027\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2-nvidia-denial\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber/Rubin Ultra delay, stating that \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-rubin-ultra-kyber-2027\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3-backup-plan-cancelled\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar power — was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its odd design and heavy operational burden.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-rubin-ultra-kyber-2027\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4-no-proven-scaleup-solution\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said the cancelled bridge-rack plan leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" and predicted that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google — whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs — a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-rubin-ultra-kyber-2027\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5-rubin-full-production-fall-ship\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to CNBC.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e6-semisanalysis-dc-revenue-estimate\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projected that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 10,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market.\",\n     \"Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-memory-price\",\n    \"question\": \"Will DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth-memory contract prices stay elevated (or keep rising) through calendar 2026, keeping memory at roughly 25–30% of an NVIDIA AI rack's bill of materials and a potential constraint on AI-system build volumes?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Direction and level of DRAM/NAND/HBM contract pricing during calendar 2026 and whether memory supply stays tight enough to constrain NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack build volumes.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2026 — memory contract pricing for this calendar year; the item gives no release date, so the period is fixed by the reason (a shared price several companies depend on for this year) and settled by memory price trackers and memory makers' quarterly reports.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-rack-price\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-memory-content\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin (VR200) rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was largely absent before. As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5–10% on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-price\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-shipment-schedule\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to the Morgan Stanley-based account in MarketScale, first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-memory-supply\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is the force pushing up component costs, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final rack pricing by $1 million or more per rack.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-price\",\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-self-source-price\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million (from about $7.8 million), showing that memory procurement structure materially moves the per-rack number by roughly $1 million or more.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-price\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 11,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion — the level Nvidia is said to project.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-1\",\n    \"question\": \"Will DRAM/memory supply stay tight in calendar 2027 and keep memory chip prices elevated, as Micron expects tight market conditions to last until 2028 and new industry capacity is not due online until 2027 or later?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The direction of DRAM/memory contract prices and supply tightness in calendar 2027 — elevated or rising further versus easing as new memory capacity comes online — an industry aggregate that sets Nvidia's memory input costs and memory availability for its GPUs.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 — set by the item: Micron says tight conditions could last until 2028 and new capacity is not online until 2027 or later, in an article dated 7 July 2026; the shared quantity takes its release data from industry contract-price trackers and memory makers' fiscal 2027 reports.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology, in the quarterly update it reported shortly before 7 July 2026, said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, according to Fool.com's Keithen Drury.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\",\n     \"clean-8\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury wrote on 7 July 2026 that memory chip prices have skyrocketed because supply has not kept up with massive data center build-out demand, and that new capacity for many in the memory industry will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"There has been strong demand for both types of memory due to the massive data center build-out, but supply hasn't kept up with this unprecedented demand. As a result, prices for memory chips have skyrocketed, allowing Micron to profit from the shortage.\",\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\",\n     \"clean-8\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology guided its next quarter (its fiscal Q4 2026, ending August 2026) to about $50 billion in revenue, which Fool.com's Keithen Drury noted is roughly half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury stated on 7 July 2026 that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 12,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Memory supply constraints and tight availability now warrant the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final pricing by $1+ million per rack\",\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"memory-prices\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials on the VR200 (from 5-10% on GB200) while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%. Morgan Stanley notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, as reported in the context of Morgan Stanley's research.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory is constrained against AI-driven demand, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, tying memory-market cost pressure to the same supply squeeze behind AI infrastructure costs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 13,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\",\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n    \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The share of surveyed Chinese enterprise AI accelerator budget going to domestic products — and by implication Nvidia's remaining share — over the 12 months after the July 2026 survey.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-10\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the U.S. government (Congress and/or the FCC) enact legislation or regulation to ban or phase out Chinese-made lidar sensors in U.S. autonomous/robotics systems, which would change the sensor options and cost competitiveness available to NVIDIA's DRIVE Hyperion autonomous-vehicle platform?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether proposed U.S. legislation/regulation to phase out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States is enacted (and its scope and timing).\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology, in its recent quarterly announcement (reported in this 7 July 2026 article), said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, alongside a quarter of huge growth and strong expectations for the following quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"fool.com (Keithen Drury) writes that memory prices have skyrocketed because supply has not kept up with data-center-driven demand, and that new industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving inventory low for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"There has been strong demand for both types of memory due to the massive data center build-out, but supply hasn't kept up with this unprecedented demand. As a result, prices for memory chips have skyrocketed, allowing Micron to profit from the shortage.\",\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and projects that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which it says will drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article states that Micron guided next-quarter revenue to $50 billion, which it says is about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate, implying a roughly $100 billion expectation for Nvidia's revenue in that period.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 14,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-mem\",\n    \"question\": \"Will memory (DRAM/HBM) contract prices stay elevated through calendar 2027 — i.e., will the memory market remain tight as Micron forecasts conditions persisting into 2028 — keeping NVIDIA's memory input costs high and potentially constraining GPU supply?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The direction and level of memory (DRAM/HBM) contract prices through calendar 2027, measured against Micron's forecast of tight conditions lasting until 2028 and low supply for at least the next year and a half.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2027; the item (published 2026-07-07) forecasts low memory supply for at least the next year and a half and tight conditions possibly until 2028, so 2027 is the first full year the forecast can be checked against TrendForce/Samsung/SK Hynix/Micron disclosures.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"fool.com's Keithen Drury reports that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026 (this year) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2a\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology, reporting another strong quarter and strong near-term expectations, said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-mem\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2b\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that memory chip prices have skyrocketed as supply lags data-center-driven demand, and that new industry capacity won't be online until 2027 or later, keeping supply low for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory prices higher still.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"As a result, prices for memory chips have skyrocketed, allowing Micron to profit from the shortage.\",\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-mem\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology guided next quarter to $50 billion in revenue, which the author notes is about half of what NVIDIA is expected to generate.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-mem\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 15,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-1\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the average contract price of DRAM (including HBM) memory be in calendar 2027 versus 2026 — will the memory shortage keep DRAM prices rising through 2027 as the item's sources expect?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The direction and level of DRAM/HBM contract prices in calendar 2027 relative to 2026: further increases consistent with tightness lasting to 2028, or a plateau/decline as new capacity comes online.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 — the item (published 2026-07-07) says new memory capacity for many in the industry will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low supply for at least the next year and a half, and that tight conditions could last until 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology, in its latest quarterly report before this 2026-07-07 article, said that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, which Fool.com's Keithen Drury describes as huge news alongside strong growth and expectations for the following quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that new memory capacity for many industry participants will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving memory inventory in low supply for at least the next year and a half, which could push memory chip prices even higher because demand for these devices is not slowing.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to Fool.com's Keithen Drury, the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that next year (2027) the AI hyperscalers' data center capital expenditures could be over $1 trillion, up from $650 billion planned for 2026; he adds this will lead to even higher demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\",\n     \"That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 16,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"G\",\n    \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-micron-fq3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron reported fiscal third-quarter 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%, as cited by columnist Allen Harris.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-nvda-margins\",\n    \"summary\": \"Columnist Allen Harris cites Nvidia's latest reported figures in comparing it with Micron: year-over-year revenue growth of about 85%, GAAP gross margin of 74.9% and an operating margin of roughly 66%, adding that Nvidia still earns more dollars than Micron. The item does not specify which quarter these Nvidia figures cover.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-goldman-ai-capex\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, as cited by columnist Allen Harris.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-apple-memory-cost\",\n    \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 17,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"G\",\n    \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-goldman-capex\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge. The spending covers chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure required to run AI at scale.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to the article's comparison with Micron, NVIDIA's latest reported quarter showed roughly 85% revenue growth, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9%, and an operating margin of about 66%, and NVIDIA still earns more dollars in absolute terms than Micron despite Micron's faster growth and fatter margins.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia remains the king of AI chips. Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, a spending category the article says includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure required to run AI at scale.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron, a memory-chip maker whose high-bandwidth memory (HBM) demand the article ties to AI accelerators such as NVIDIA's, reported fiscal Q3 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion, up about 346% from $9.30 billion a year earlier; GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion a year earlier; a gross margin of 84.6% (up from 37.7%) and an operating margin of 80.4% (up from 23.3%).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"In Micron’s case, the math is stunning. For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 18,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\",\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n    \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The share of surveyed Chinese enterprise AI accelerator budget going to domestic products — and by implication Nvidia's remaining share — over the 12 months after the July 2026 survey.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-10\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the U.S. government (Congress and/or the FCC) enact legislation or regulation to ban or phase out Chinese-made lidar sensors in U.S. autonomous/robotics systems, which would change the sensor options and cost competitiveness available to NVIDIA's DRIVE Hyperion autonomous-vehicle platform?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether proposed U.S. legislation/regulation to phase out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States is enacted (and its scope and timing).\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology announced another quarter of large growth and gave very strong guidance for the following quarter, and said it expects tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, per a Fool.com article by Keithen Drury. If the tightness lasts that long, it implies a longer period of elevated memory supply scarcity affecting buyers such as Nvidia.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology guided revenue for its next quarter to $50 billion, which the article says is about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article's author writes that new memory capacity for many industry players will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory supply for at least the next year and a half and potentially driving memory chip prices even higher as demand does not slow.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to the article, AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditure this year, and next year that figure could be over $1 trillion, which the author says will raise demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\",\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 19,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The four largest AI hyperscalers plan to spend roughly $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the fool.com analysis, which describes this planned spend as the baseline for a sharp increase it expects next year (published 2026-07-07).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), up from around $650 billion planned for 2026, as reported by fool.com (published 2026-07-07).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026 (\\\"this year\\\"). No individual hyperscaler is named as the source of the aggregate figure.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), as reported by the fool.com article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 20,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K1\",\n    \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K2\",\n    \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"NH1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"NH1\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-1\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its next-generation Vera Rubin Ultra chips and had been slated to debut in 2027 — reach first shipments in calendar 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmed first shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack / Vera Rubin Ultra systems in calendar 2027, versus a slip into 2028 (or a materially limited-volume launch).\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 — set by the item's own timeline: Kyber was slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027 and is reported delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, so end-2027 settles whether it shipped in 2027.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a post that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet designed to house Nvidia's 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system remains difficult to manufacture. SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has slipped to 2028, saying \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia scrapped a backup plan to bolt two of its current-generation racks together to match Rubin Ultra's scale, after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on the design as awkward and operationally burdensome; the firm said this leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra\\\" and predicted the gap could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to CNBC.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 21,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"In a fool.com column, contributor Keithen Drury wrote that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures this year (2026), and that next year (2027) that figure could be over $1 trillion; he infers this would drive even higher demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology said in announcing its latest quarterly results that memory market conditions are tight and could last until 2028; the fool.com article adds that new memory-industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory supply for at least the next year and a half and possibly pushing memory chip prices even higher as demand does not slow.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\",\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology guided next quarter (the fiscal quarter following the one ending in August 2026) to $50 billion in revenue, a figure the fool.com author describes as about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 22,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K1\",\n    \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K2\",\n    \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"NH1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"E3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"NH1\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-kyber\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture (the NVL144 144-GPU cabinet that was slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra) begin shipping in calendar 2027 as originally scheduled, or slip into 2028?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack-scale systems have shipped in calendar 2027 versus a slip to 2028 (or later), and whether the PCB-midplane manufacturability problem is resolved.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 — set by the item: Kyber had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, and SemiAnalysis reports it delayed more than 12 months to 2028, so the check date is whether shipments occur by end-2027.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, which had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system remains difficult to manufacture. Nvidia rejected the report.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture is delayed to 2028, saying \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVL576 — the larger Nvidia system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"A backup plan to bolt two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate, according to SemiAnalysis.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said the cancelled workaround leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" and predicted this could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the AI market.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-kyber\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-6\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to CNBC.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-7\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 23,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-memory-price\",\n    \"question\": \"How high will DRAM/HBM memory contract prices run in calendar 2027, the memory input that NVIDIA's GPUs depend on, given Micron's forecast that tight market conditions last until 2028 and that new memory capacity arrives only in 2027 or later?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of industry DRAM and HBM contract prices in calendar 2027, measured against the tight-market baseline the item describes (memory prices already 'skyrocketed', tightness forecast into 2028).\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 — next calendar year at the item's publication (2026-07-07), which forecasts low memory supply for 'at least the next year and a half' and tight conditions potentially until 2028.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-hyperscaler-capex\",\n    \"summary\": \"In his fool.com article, Keithen Drury wrote that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026) and that next year the figure could be over $1 trillion, which he says would raise demand for memory chips as well as for NVIDIA GPUs. The $1 trillion figure is the author's projection, not attributed to NVIDIA in this item.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\",\n     \"That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-memory-tightness\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology said in its latest quarterly announcement that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, and the article's author added that new memory capacity for many in the industry will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low memory inventory for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\",\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-price\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-micron-guidance\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology guided its next fiscal quarter (fiscal Q4 2026, which ends in August 2026) to $50 billion of revenue; the article's author noted that figure is about half of what NVIDIA is expected to generate, an implied NVIDIA expectation of roughly $100 billion that the item does not state explicitly.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\",\n     \"Micron operates on a non-standard fiscal year calendar, and its FY 2026 ends in August.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-price\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 24,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\",\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n    \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The share of surveyed Chinese enterprise AI accelerator budget going to domestic products — and by implication Nvidia's remaining share — over the 12 months after the July 2026 survey.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-10\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the U.S. government (Congress and/or the FCC) enact legislation or regulation to ban or phase out Chinese-made lidar sensors in U.S. autonomous/robotics systems, which would change the sensor options and cost competitiveness available to NVIDIA's DRIVE Hyperion autonomous-vehicle platform?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether proposed U.S. legislation/regulation to phase out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States is enacted (and its scope and timing).\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology reported another strong quarter with large growth and high expectations for the following quarter, and said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article forecasts that new memory (DRAM and NAND) capacity at many producers will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving inventory supply low for at least the next year and a half and pushing memory chip prices even higher as demand stays strong.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026), and that next year (2027) the figure could be over $1 trillion, which it says will drive even higher demand for memory chips and for Nvidia GPUs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron guided next-quarter revenue to $50 billion, which the author says is about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate in the same period.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 25,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"G\",\n    \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-micron-q3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron reported fiscal third-quarter 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion, up from $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion a year earlier, a gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and an operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%. Micron is a memory (HBM/DRAM/NAND) supplier whose results reflect the memory pricing environment that is an input cost for Nvidia's AI systems.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"In Micron’s case, the math is stunning. For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-nvda-margins\",\n    \"summary\": \"In a Micron-versus-Nvidia margin comparison, the author states that Nvidia's latest revenue growth was about 85% (against Micron's ~346%), with Nvidia's GAAP gross margin at 74.9% and operating margin roughly 66% (against Micron's 84.6% and 80.4%). The exact period of Nvidia's results is not specified in the item.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-goldman-capex\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, spanning chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-apple-memory\",\n    \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-5\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 26,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-1\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for the memory going into NVIDIA's racks (DRAM/LPDDR5X, 3D NAND, HBM) average over calendar 2027, and how much memory cost per Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will that imply versus the ~$2 million per rack Morgan Stanley estimates for the first shipments?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The average contract price of DRAM/LPDDR5X/NAND (and HBM) bought for NVIDIA AI racks in calendar 2027 and the resulting memory content per VR200 rack, measured against the ~$2 million per rack / 25-30% of bill of materials Morgan Stanley estimates now.\",\n    \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 average contract prices, checked at the memory contract-price survey releases through 2027 — set by the reason (a shared input price outside any one company); the item gives no specific release date, so the check is the 2027 series of contract-price releases.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; the analysis says the GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content for NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5 to 10 percent on GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on VR200 while the GPU's share fell from about 63 percent to 51 percent.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n     \"As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products; it advises treating memory supply as a scheduling risk for AI rack buyers because memory is a quarter or more of rack value with supply tight.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Only a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrained supply of DRAM, NAND, and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, is the same one now raising component costs across the market. Contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n     \"Treat memory supply as a scheduling risk, not only a cost. With memory representing a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the price of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 rack down to around $6.7 million from the roughly $7.8 million estimate, i.e. procurement structure can move final pricing by more than $1 million per rack.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to the article's account of Morgan Stanley's analysis, NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 racks are scheduled to begin first shipments in the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 27,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K1\",\n    \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"K2\",\n    \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture, the rack-scale system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture, and that the larger NVL576 system (eight racks linked optically) is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes. SemiAnalysis concluded this leaves NVIDIA with no proven solution to expand scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra, which it said could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-nvl144\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber/Rubin Ultra delay, stating that \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"new-kyber-nvl144\",\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"new-1\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra generation — the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system that the item says was slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027 — begin shipping by the end of 2027, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack-scale system by end-2027 (roadmap intact, as NVIDIA says) versus a slip into 2028 (as SemiAnalysis reports).\",\n    \"period\": \"2027-12-31 — end of the year the item says Kyber was slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra; SemiAnalysis reports a >12-month slip to 2028 while NVIDIA says its roadmap is intact.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a post that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture — the rack-scale system slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane that connects modules remains difficult to manufacture; the larger NVL576 system linking eight racks via optical connections is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay, stating \\\"Our roadmap is intact\\\" — i.e., the company maintains its product schedule, including the 2027 Kyber / Rubin Ultra debut the report says has slipped to 2028.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar power and scale-up capacity — was cancelled after heavy pushback from cloud service providers and hyperscalers over its odd design and operational burden, leaving NVIDIA with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra\\\" and, in the firm's view, a rare technical opening at the high end for Advanced Micro Devices and Google.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are to begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, CNBC reported.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 28,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"G\",\n    \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\",\n    \"year\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-goldman-capex\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge. The spending covers chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure required to run AI at scale.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of guarantees, credit support or similar contingent exposure NVIDIA discloses for AI cloud partners; zero if none.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform, confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An official Chinese government/regulator decision permitting (or continuing to bar) H200 sales in China, confirmed by an official Chinese announcement or by NVIDIA disclosing H200 China revenue/shipments.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 data-center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against the ~$650 billion plan reported in the item and NVIDIA's ~$1 trillion projection for 2027.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system (paired with Rubin Ultra), confirmed by NVIDIA disclosure or an official shipment announcement, occurring in 2027 versus slipping to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the $119 billion figure reported in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Combined calendar-2026 spending by the big four hyperscalers on custom AI accelerators.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"In an opinion column comparing Micron with NVIDIA, columnist Allen Harris cited NVIDIA's own latest reported metrics: revenue growth of about 85%, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9% and an operating margin of roughly 66%, adding that NVIDIA still earns more dollars than Micron. The item does not specify which NVIDIA quarter these figures cover.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Micron’s latest revenue growth was about 346 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 85 percent. Micron’s GAAP gross margin was 84.6 percent, compared with Nvidia’s 74.9 percent. Micron’s operating margin was 80.4 percent, compared with Nvidia’s roughly 66 percent. Nvidia still earns more dollars.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology reported, per columnist Allen Harris, fiscal third-quarter 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, a gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and an operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%. Micron is a memory/HBM supplier into AI accelerators, so its reported margins bear on memory pricing and input costs for AI systems.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"In Micron’s case, the math is stunning. For its third fiscal quarter of 2026, Micron reported revenue of $41.46 billion. One year earlier, revenue was $9.30 billion. That is roughly 346 percent revenue growth. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles) net income was $28.24 billion, compared with $1.89 billion a year ago. Micron’s gross margin was 84.6 percent, up from 37.7 percent a year earlier, and its operating margin was 80.4 percent, up from 23.3 percent.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and related infrastructure, as cited by columnist Allen Harris.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"The broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs, as cited by columnist Allen Harris. This reports memory cost inflation reaching an end-device maker's pricing, a datapoint on memory input costs in AI hardware supply chains; no price amounts or dates are given.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 29,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year, up from the group's planned spend of around $650 billion this year, according to a fool.com article.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\",\n     \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The amount of credit support / backstop commitments NVIDIA reports backing AI cloud partners' compute procurement and the resulting credit-risk exposure.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The reported CPU:GPU ratio for AI inference workloads in 2026, and whether it has moved materially from 1:8 toward 1:1.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Confirmation that first Vera Rubin shipments have begun, versus slipping into 2027 or later.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2026 output of Huawei-designed AI chips manufactured at SMIC, in units, measured against the contested estimates in circulation (Nvidia's report: SMIC above 700,000 Ascend 910C by end-2026 at a 50,000-per-month run rate and several million 910B in 2027; Commerce Secretary Lutnick: 200,000 advanced chips for 2025; Chris McGuire: 800,000 910C in 2025, 2 million in 2026 and 4 million in 2027 as stress cases).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether H200 sales to Chinese customers take place (China data-center revenue returning above zero) versus Beijing keeping Nvidia locked out despite the U.S. approval.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined 2027 data center capex of the big four AI hyperscalers, measured against Nvidia's ~$1 trillion projection and the 2026 level of ~$650 billion.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"First shipments of the Kyber NVL144 rack system (Rubin Ultra generation) in 2028 versus the original 2027 schedule, and whether the larger NVL576 multi-rack system ships in volume at all.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The dollar amount of NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments disclosed at its next annual report, measured against the ~$119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), while the article says the same group plans to spend around $650 billion this year (2026).\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 30,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C26\",\n    \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\",\n    \"year\": \"2026\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"C27\",\n    \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\",\n    \"year\": \"2027\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   {\n    \"stored_id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion planned this year (2026), as reported by the article. Both are forward projections/plans, not disclosed actual results.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"stored_links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\",\n  \"open_theses\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n    \"question\": \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding amount of credit support, guarantees or backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model, disclosed at its next annual report.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin reaches first shipments (start of revenue) by the end of 2026.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n    \"question\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditure for calendar 2027, as disclosed in their full-year results.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n    \"question\": \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The level and direction of contract memory (DRAM, NAND, HBM) prices in 2026-2027 and the resulting per-rack memory content cost (about $2 million on the VR200).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n    \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 / Rubin Ultra rack platform reaches first shipments (the step that brings revenue) in 2027 or is delayed to 2028.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n    \"question\": \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The outstanding supply/purchase commitments disclosed in NVIDIA's next annual report, compared with the $119 billion cited in this item.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-8\",\n    \"question\": \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The combined calendar-2026 revenue (or disclosed shipment volume) of the four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerator programmes, as disclosed in their full-year 2026 results and earnings calls.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"clean-9\",\n    \"question\": \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The share of surveyed Chinese enterprise AI accelerator budget going to domestic products — and by implication Nvidia's remaining share — over the 12 months after the July 2026 survey.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"new_theses\": [],\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article's author states that, in 2026 (the year of the 7 July 2026 article), the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures .\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia claims that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), as reported by fool.com; the figure is Nvidia's projection, reported second-hand rather than a hyperscaler disclosure.\",\n    \"source_passages\": [\n     \"Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\"\n    ],\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"packet-claims-1.json\\\"));\nfor x in p:\n print(json.dumps({\\\"n\\\":x[\\\"n\\\"],\\\"claims\\\":x[\\\"claims\\\"],\\\"refs\\\":[v[\\\"summary\\\"] for v in x[\\\"stored_reference_items\\\"]],\\\"rule\\\":x[\\\"link_rule\\\"],\\\"usable\\\":x[\\\"answer_usable\\\"],\\\"theses\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":t[\\\"id\\\"],\\\"q\\\":t[\\\"question\\\"]} for t in x[\\\"open_theses\\\"]+x[\\\"new_theses\\\"] if any(w in t[\\\"question\\\"].lower() for w in [\\\"capex\\\",\\\"capital expenditure\\\",\\\"kyber\\\",\\\"infrastructure spending\\\"])],\\\"evidence\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":e[\\\"id\\\"],\\\"s\\\":e[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"links\\\":e[\\\"links\\\"]} for e in x[\\\"kept_evidence\\\"]]},ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"packet-claims-1.json\\\"));\nfor x in p:\n print(json.dumps({\\\"n\\\":x[\\\"n\\\"],\\\"claims\\\":x[\\\"claims\\\"],\\\"refs\\\":[v[\\\"summary\\\"] for v in x[\\\"stored_reference_items\\\"]],\\\"rule\\\":x[\\\"link_rule\\\"],\\\"usable\\\":x[\\\"answer_usable\\\"],\\\"theses\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":t[\\\"id\\\"],\\\"q\\\":t[\\\"question\\\"]} for t in x[\\\"open_theses\\\"]+x[\\\"new_theses\\\"] if any(w in t[\\\"question\\\"].lower() for w in [\\\"capex\\\",\\\"capital expenditure\\\",\\\"kyber\\\",\\\"infrastructure spending\\\"])],\\\"evidence\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":e[\\\"id\\\"],\\\"s\\\":e[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"links\\\":e[\\\"links\\\"]} for e in x[\\\"kept_evidence\\\"]]},ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"{\"n\": 1, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the cabinet designed to house 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, packing 144 GPUs per unit — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane that connects the system's modules remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint. The design had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027. Nvidia rejected the report (see separate item).\", \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed, stating that its roadmap is intact.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}, {\"id\": \"new-kyber\", \"q\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its Vera Rubin Ultra chips — reach first shipments by the end of calendar 2028, following the reported delay of more than 12 months from its planned 2027 debut?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-kyber-delay\", \"s\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, citing manufacturing difficulty with the specialised multi-layer PCB midplane that connects electronic modules in the system.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-nvl576\", \"s\": \"SemiAnalysis also said NVL576 — a larger NVIDIA system linking eight racks via optical connections — is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-backup-plan-scrapped\", \"s\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar power/scale — was scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers rejected the design as awkward and operationally burdensome, leaving NVIDIA with no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra; the firm predicted that could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-nvidia-denial\", \"s\": \"NVIDIA rejected the SemiAnalysis report on the Kyber delay and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-rubin-shipping\", \"s\": \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-dc-revenue-estimate\", \"s\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"links\": []}]}\n{\"n\": 2, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the cabinet designed to house 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, packing 144 GPUs per unit — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane that connects the system's modules remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint. The design had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027. Nvidia rejected the report (see separate item).\", \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed, stating that its roadmap is intact.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}, {\"id\": \"new-1\", \"q\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the system that debuts with Vera Rubin Ultra and carries the 2027 high-end AI rack cycle — begin shipping (the step that brings revenue) in calendar 2027, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis projects?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"s\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis said in a post that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture — the rack-scale system slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027 — has been delayed to 2028 because its specialized multi-layer PCB midplane remains challenging to manufacture, and that the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\", \"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"s\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed to 2028, stating \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"s\": \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, CNBC reported.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"s\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA's backup plan of bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar scale-up performance was scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers rejected the design as odd and operationally burdensome, leaving NVIDIA with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra\\\" — which the firm said could give Advanced Micro Devices and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-5\", \"s\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"links\": []}]}\n{\"n\": 3, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"The four largest AI hyperscalers plan to spend roughly $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the fool.com analysis, which describes this planned spend as the baseline for a sharp increase it expects next year (published 2026-07-07).\", \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), up from around $650 billion planned for 2026, as reported by fool.com (published 2026-07-07).\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"q\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}, {\"id\": \"new-1\", \"q\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers (Amazon, Google, Microsoft, Meta) spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027, and does their combined spend approach the >$1 trillion NVIDIA projects for the group?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"s\": \"According to fool.com (The Motley Fool), the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and NVIDIA claims the group will spend more than $1 trillion in 2027. These are a plan and a projection, not realized spending figures.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\", \"new-1\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 4, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"The four largest AI hyperscalers plan to spend roughly $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the fool.com analysis, which describes this planned spend as the baseline for a sharp increase it expects next year (published 2026-07-07).\", \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), up from around $650 billion planned for 2026, as reported by fool.com (published 2026-07-07).\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"q\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"s\": \"fool.com reports that in the current year (calendar 2026) the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"s\": \"Nvidia claims that next year (calendar 2027) the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures, up from roughly $650 billion planned for this year, according to fool.com.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 5, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"q\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"}, {\"id\": \"new-kyber-2027\", \"q\": \"Will Nvidia's Rubin Ultra rack-scale generation — the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture and the larger NVL576 system — begin shipping in calendar 2027 as planned, rather than slipping more than 12 months to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"s\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture — the server cabinet designed to house 144 GPUs and slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer printed circuit board (PCB) midplane at the heart of the system remains difficult to manufacture.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber-2027\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"s\": \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed to 2028, stating \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber-2027\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"s\": \"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia's NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely to be delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber-2027\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"s\": \"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to deliver comparable scale-up capacity — has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its awkward design and heavy operational burden, leaving Nvidia with, in the firm's words, \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra\\\"; SemiAnalysis predicted this could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber-2027\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"s\": \"The report states that Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping this fall (fall 2026) to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e6\", \"s\": \"SemiAnalysis projected that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"links\": []}]}\n{\"n\": 6, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.\"], \"rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"q\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"q\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"s\": \"Nvidia's revenue grew about 85%, with a GAAP gross margin of 74.9% and an operating margin of roughly 66%, according to the column's comparison of Nvidia with Micron's latest quarter; the period and quarter for Nvidia's figures are not specified in the item.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"s\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, a figure that includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other AI plumbing.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"s\": \"Memory supplier Micron reported fiscal Q3 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion, up from $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, GAAP gross margin of 84.6% (up from 37.7%) and operating margin of 80.4% (up from 23.3%); the column says rising memory-company margins mean higher costs for device makers, server makers and cloud providers.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"s\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\", \"links\": []}]}\n{\"n\": 7, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion — the level Nvidia is said to project.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"q\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"q\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"s\": \"fool.com's Keithen Drury wrote that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he said would drive even higher demand for memory chips and for Nvidia GPUs.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"s\": \"Micron announced that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028; the article adds that new industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory supply for at least the next year and a half and potentially driving memory chip prices even higher as AI data-center demand persists.\", \"links\": []}]}\n{\"n\": 8, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\", \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\", \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"s\": \"Morgan Stanley Research, reported by MarketScale, estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\", \"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"s\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; as a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5–10% on the GB200 to 25–30% on the VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\", \"mem-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"s\": \"NVIDIA's Vera Rubin first shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, according to the item.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"s\": \"The item reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", \"links\": [\"mem-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-5\", \"s\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the standard estimate, a reminder that procurement structure materially moves the number.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\", \"mem-1\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 9, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"q\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"}, {\"id\": \"new-rubin-ultra-kyber-2027\", \"q\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack designed to house its Rubin Ultra chips and carry its high-end data-center value — begin shipping by end of calendar 2027 as originally slated, or slip to 2028 (or later) as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1-kyber-delay\", \"s\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture — the NVL144 cabinet that packs 144 of Nvidia's most powerful chips and had been slated to debut with the Vera Rubin Ultra system in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis added that NVL576, a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"links\": [\"new-rubin-ultra-kyber-2027\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2-nvidia-denial\", \"s\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber/Rubin Ultra delay, stating that \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", \"links\": [\"new-rubin-ultra-kyber-2027\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3-backup-plan-cancelled\", \"s\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar power — was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its odd design and heavy operational burden.\", \"links\": [\"new-rubin-ultra-kyber-2027\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4-no-proven-scaleup-solution\", \"s\": \"SemiAnalysis said the cancelled bridge-rack plan leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" and predicted that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google — whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs — a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\", \"links\": [\"new-rubin-ultra-kyber-2027\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5-rubin-full-production-fall-ship\", \"s\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to CNBC.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e6-semisanalysis-dc-revenue-estimate\", \"s\": \"SemiAnalysis projected that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"links\": []}]}\n{\"n\": 10, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\", \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"q\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-rack-price\", \"s\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-memory-content\", \"s\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin (VR200) rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was largely absent before. As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5–10% on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\", \"links\": [\"new-memory-price\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-shipment-schedule\", \"s\": \"According to the Morgan Stanley-based account in MarketScale, first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-memory-supply\", \"s\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is the force pushing up component costs, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final rack pricing by $1 million or more per rack.\", \"links\": [\"new-memory-price\", \"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-self-source-price\", \"s\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million (from about $7.8 million), showing that memory procurement structure materially moves the per-rack number by roughly $1 million or more.\", \"links\": [\"new-memory-price\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 11, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion — the level Nvidia is said to project.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"q\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"q\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"s\": \"Micron Technology, in the quarterly update it reported shortly before 7 July 2026, said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, according to Fool.com's Keithen Drury.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\", \"clean-8\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"s\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury wrote on 7 July 2026 that memory chip prices have skyrocketed because supply has not kept up with massive data center build-out demand, and that new capacity for many in the memory industry will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\", \"clean-8\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"s\": \"Micron Technology guided its next quarter (its fiscal Q4 2026, ending August 2026) to about $50 billion in revenue, which Fool.com's Keithen Drury noted is roughly half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"s\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury stated on 7 July 2026 that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 12, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\", \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\", \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"s\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials on the VR200 (from 5-10% on GB200) while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%. Morgan Stanley notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"s\": \"The article reports that first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, as reported in the context of Morgan Stanley's research.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"s\": \"The article reports that supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory is constrained against AI-driven demand, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, tying memory-market cost pressure to the same supply squeeze behind AI infrastructure costs.\", \"links\": []}]}\n{\"n\": 13, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"q\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"s\": \"Micron Technology, in its recent quarterly announcement (reported in this 7 July 2026 article), said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, alongside a quarter of huge growth and strong expectations for the following quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"s\": \"fool.com (Keithen Drury) writes that memory prices have skyrocketed because supply has not kept up with data-center-driven demand, and that new industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving inventory low for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"s\": \"The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and projects that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which it says will drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"s\": \"The article states that Micron guided next-quarter revenue to $50 billion, which it says is about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate, implying a roughly $100 billion expectation for Nvidia's revenue in that period.\", \"links\": []}]}\n{\"n\": 14, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"q\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"s\": \"fool.com's Keithen Drury reports that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026 (this year) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2a\", \"s\": \"Micron Technology, reporting another strong quarter and strong near-term expectations, said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028.\", \"links\": [\"new-mem\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2b\", \"s\": \"The article reports that memory chip prices have skyrocketed as supply lags data-center-driven demand, and that new industry capacity won't be online until 2027 or later, keeping supply low for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory prices higher still.\", \"links\": [\"new-mem\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"s\": \"Micron Technology guided next quarter to $50 billion in revenue, which the author notes is about half of what NVIDIA is expected to generate.\", \"links\": [\"new-mem\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 15, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"q\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"s\": \"Micron Technology, in its latest quarterly report before this 2026-07-07 article, said that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, which Fool.com's Keithen Drury describes as huge news alongside strong growth and expectations for the following quarter.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"s\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that new memory capacity for many industry participants will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving memory inventory in low supply for at least the next year and a half, which could push memory chip prices even higher because demand for these devices is not slowing.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"s\": \"According to Fool.com's Keithen Drury, the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026).\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"s\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that next year (2027) the AI hyperscalers' data center capital expenditures could be over $1 trillion, up from $650 billion planned for 2026; he adds this will lead to even higher demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 16, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\"], \"rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"q\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-micron-fq3\", \"s\": \"Micron reported fiscal third-quarter 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%, as cited by columnist Allen Harris.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-nvda-margins\", \"s\": \"Columnist Allen Harris cites Nvidia's latest reported figures in comparing it with Micron: year-over-year revenue growth of about 85%, GAAP gross margin of 74.9% and an operating margin of roughly 66%, adding that Nvidia still earns more dollars than Micron. The item does not specify which quarter these Nvidia figures cover.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev-goldman-ai-capex\", \"s\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, as cited by columnist Allen Harris.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-apple-memory-cost\", \"s\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 17, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge. The spending covers chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure required to run AI at scale.\"], \"rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"q\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"s\": \"According to the article's comparison with Micron, NVIDIA's latest reported quarter showed roughly 85% revenue growth, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9%, and an operating margin of about 66%, and NVIDIA still earns more dollars in absolute terms than Micron despite Micron's faster growth and fatter margins.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"s\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, a spending category the article says includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure required to run AI at scale.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"s\": \"Micron, a memory-chip maker whose high-bandwidth memory (HBM) demand the article ties to AI accelerators such as NVIDIA's, reported fiscal Q3 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion, up about 346% from $9.30 billion a year earlier; GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion a year earlier; a gross margin of 84.6% (up from 37.7%) and an operating margin of 80.4% (up from 23.3%).\", \"links\": []}]}\n{\"n\": 18, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"q\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"s\": \"Micron Technology announced another quarter of large growth and gave very strong guidance for the following quarter, and said it expects tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, per a Fool.com article by Keithen Drury. If the tightness lasts that long, it implies a longer period of elevated memory supply scarcity affecting buyers such as Nvidia.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"s\": \"Micron Technology guided revenue for its next quarter to $50 billion, which the article says is about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"s\": \"The article's author writes that new memory capacity for many industry players will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory supply for at least the next year and a half and potentially driving memory chip prices even higher as demand does not slow.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"s\": \"According to the article, AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditure this year, and next year that figure could be over $1 trillion, which the author says will raise demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\", \"clean-5\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 19, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"The four largest AI hyperscalers plan to spend roughly $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the fool.com analysis, which describes this planned spend as the baseline for a sharp increase it expects next year (published 2026-07-07).\", \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), up from around $650 billion planned for 2026, as reported by fool.com (published 2026-07-07).\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"q\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"s\": \"The article reports that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026 (\\\"this year\\\"). No individual hyperscaler is named as the source of the aggregate figure.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"s\": \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), as reported by the fool.com article.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 20, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"q\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"}, {\"id\": \"new-1\", \"q\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its next-generation Vera Rubin Ultra chips and had been slated to debut in 2027 — reach first shipments in calendar 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"s\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a post that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet designed to house Nvidia's 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system remains difficult to manufacture. SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"s\": \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has slipped to 2028, saying \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"s\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia scrapped a backup plan to bolt two of its current-generation racks together to match Rubin Ultra's scale, after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on the design as awkward and operationally burdensome; the firm said this leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra\\\" and predicted the gap could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"s\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to CNBC.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"s\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"links\": []}]}\n{\"n\": 21, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"q\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"s\": \"In a fool.com column, contributor Keithen Drury wrote that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures this year (2026), and that next year (2027) that figure could be over $1 trillion; he infers this would drive even higher demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"s\": \"Micron Technology said in announcing its latest quarterly results that memory market conditions are tight and could last until 2028; the fool.com article adds that new memory-industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory supply for at least the next year and a half and possibly pushing memory chip prices even higher as demand does not slow.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"s\": \"Micron Technology guided next quarter (the fiscal quarter following the one ending in August 2026) to $50 billion in revenue, a figure the fool.com author describes as about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\", \"links\": []}]}\n{\"n\": 22, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"q\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"}, {\"id\": \"new-kyber\", \"q\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture (the NVL144 144-GPU cabinet that was slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra) begin shipping in calendar 2027 as originally scheduled, or slip into 2028?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"s\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, which had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system remains difficult to manufacture. Nvidia rejected the report.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"s\": \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture is delayed to 2028, saying \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\", \"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"s\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVL576 — the larger Nvidia system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"s\": \"A backup plan to bolt two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate, according to SemiAnalysis.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-5\", \"s\": \"SemiAnalysis said the cancelled workaround leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" and predicted this could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the AI market.\", \"links\": [\"new-kyber\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-6\", \"s\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to CNBC.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-7\", \"s\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"links\": []}]}\n{\"n\": 23, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"q\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-hyperscaler-capex\", \"s\": \"In his fool.com article, Keithen Drury wrote that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026) and that next year the figure could be over $1 trillion, which he says would raise demand for memory chips as well as for NVIDIA GPUs. The $1 trillion figure is the author's projection, not attributed to NVIDIA in this item.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-memory-tightness\", \"s\": \"Micron Technology said in its latest quarterly announcement that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, and the article's author added that new memory capacity for many in the industry will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low memory inventory for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher.\", \"links\": [\"new-memory-price\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-micron-guidance\", \"s\": \"Micron Technology guided its next fiscal quarter (fiscal Q4 2026, which ends in August 2026) to $50 billion of revenue; the article's author noted that figure is about half of what NVIDIA is expected to generate, an implied NVIDIA expectation of roughly $100 billion that the item does not state explicitly.\", \"links\": [\"new-memory-price\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 24, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"q\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"s\": \"Micron Technology reported another strong quarter with large growth and high expectations for the following quarter, and said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"s\": \"The article forecasts that new memory (DRAM and NAND) capacity at many producers will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving inventory supply low for at least the next year and a half and pushing memory chip prices even higher as demand stays strong.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"s\": \"The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026), and that next year (2027) the figure could be over $1 trillion, which it says will drive even higher demand for memory chips and for Nvidia GPUs.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"s\": \"Micron guided next-quarter revenue to $50 billion, which the author says is about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate in the same period.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 25, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\"], \"rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"q\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-micron-q3\", \"s\": \"Micron reported fiscal third-quarter 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion, up from $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion a year earlier, a gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and an operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%. Micron is a memory (HBM/DRAM/NAND) supplier whose results reflect the memory pricing environment that is an input cost for Nvidia's AI systems.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-nvda-margins\", \"s\": \"In a Micron-versus-Nvidia margin comparison, the author states that Nvidia's latest revenue growth was about 85% (against Micron's ~346%), with Nvidia's GAAP gross margin at 74.9% and operating margin roughly 66% (against Micron's 84.6% and 80.4%). The exact period of Nvidia's results is not specified in the item.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"ev-goldman-capex\", \"s\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, spanning chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-apple-memory\", \"s\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\", \"links\": [\"clean-5\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 26, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"q\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"q\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"s\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; the analysis says the GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"s\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content for NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5 to 10 percent on GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on VR200 while the GPU's share fell from about 63 percent to 51 percent.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"s\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products; it advises treating memory supply as a scheduling risk for AI rack buyers because memory is a quarter or more of rack value with supply tight.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"s\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the price of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 rack down to around $6.7 million from the roughly $7.8 million estimate, i.e. procurement structure can move final pricing by more than $1 million per rack.\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"s\": \"According to the article's account of Morgan Stanley's analysis, NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 racks are scheduled to begin first shipments in the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", \"links\": [\"clean-3\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 27, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture, the rack-scale system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture, and that the larger NVL576 system (eight racks linked optically) is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes. SemiAnalysis concluded this leaves NVIDIA with no proven solution to expand scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra, which it said could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\", \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber/Rubin Ultra delay, stating that \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}, {\"id\": \"new-1\", \"q\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra generation — the Kyber NVL144 rack-scale system that the item says was slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027 — begin shipping by the end of 2027, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"s\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a post that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture — the rack-scale system slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane that connects modules remains difficult to manufacture; the larger NVL576 system linking eight racks via optical connections is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"s\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay, stating \\\"Our roadmap is intact\\\" — i.e., the company maintains its product schedule, including the 2027 Kyber / Rubin Ultra debut the report says has slipped to 2028.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"s\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar power and scale-up capacity — was cancelled after heavy pushback from cloud service providers and hyperscalers over its odd design and operational burden, leaving NVIDIA with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra\\\" and, in the firm's view, a rare technical opening at the high end for Advanced Micro Devices and Google.\", \"links\": [\"new-1\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"s\": \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are to begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, CNBC reported.\", \"links\": [\"clean-2\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-5\", \"s\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 28, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge. The spending covers chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure required to run AI at scale.\"], \"rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"q\": \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"s\": \"In an opinion column comparing Micron with NVIDIA, columnist Allen Harris cited NVIDIA's own latest reported metrics: revenue growth of about 85%, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9% and an operating margin of roughly 66%, adding that NVIDIA still earns more dollars than Micron. The item does not specify which NVIDIA quarter these figures cover.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"s\": \"Micron Technology reported, per columnist Allen Harris, fiscal third-quarter 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (about 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, a gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and an operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%. Micron is a memory/HBM supplier into AI accelerators, so its reported margins bear on memory pricing and input costs for AI systems.\", \"links\": []}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"s\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and related infrastructure, as cited by columnist Allen Harris.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"s\": \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs, as cited by columnist Allen Harris. This reports memory cost inflation reaching an end-device maker's pricing, a datapoint on memory input costs in AI hardware supply chains; no price amounts or dates are given.\", \"links\": []}]}\n{\"n\": 29, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year, up from the group's planned spend of around $650 billion this year, according to a fool.com article.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"q\": \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"q\": \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"s\": \"Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), while the article says the same group plans to spend around $650 billion this year (2026).\", \"links\": [\"clean-6\"]}]}\n{\"n\": 30, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion planned this year (2026), as reported by the article. Both are forward projections/plans, not disclosed actual results.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [{\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"q\": \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"q\": \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"}], \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"s\": \"The article's author states that, in 2026 (the year of the 7 July 2026 article), the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"s\": \"Nvidia claims that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), as reported by fool.com; the figure is Nvidia's projection, reported second-hand rather than a hyperscaler disclosure.\", \"links\": [\"clean-4\"]}]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"packet-claims-1.json\\\"));\nfor x in p[7:22]:\n print(json.dumps({\\\"n\\\":x[\\\"n\\\"],\\\"claims\\\":x[\\\"claims\\\"],\\\"refs\\\":[v[\\\"summary\\\"] for v in x[\\\"stored_reference_items\\\"]],\\\"rule\\\":x[\\\"link_rule\\\"],\\\"usable\\\":x[\\\"answer_usable\\\"],\\\"theses\\\":[(t[\\\"id\\\"],t[\\\"question\\\"]) for t in x[\\\"open_theses\\\"]+x[\\\"new_theses\\\"]],\\\"evidence\\\":[(e[\\\"id\\\"],e[\\\"summary\\\"],e[\\\"links\\\"]) for e in x[\\\"kept_evidence\\\"]]},ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"packet-claims-1.json\\\"));\nfor x in p[7:22]:\n print(json.dumps({\\\"n\\\":x[\\\"n\\\"],\\\"claims\\\":x[\\\"claims\\\"],\\\"refs\\\":[v[\\\"summary\\\"] for v in x[\\\"stored_reference_items\\\"]],\\\"rule\\\":x[\\\"link_rule\\\"],\\\"usable\\\":x[\\\"answer_usable\\\"],\\\"theses\\\":[(t[\\\"id\\\"],t[\\\"question\\\"]) for t in x[\\\"open_theses\\\"]+x[\\\"new_theses\\\"]],\\\"evidence\\\":[(e[\\\"id\\\"],e[\\\"summary\\\"],e[\\\"links\\\"]) for e in x[\\\"kept_evidence\\\"]]},ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"{\"n\": 8, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\", \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\", \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [[\"clean-1\", \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\"], [\"clean-2\", \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\"], [\"clean-3\", \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\"], [\"clean-4\", \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"], [\"mem-1\", \"How much will memory (DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory) contract prices rise over calendar 2026–2027, pushing up the memory cost embedded in AI infrastructure and devices that NVIDIA and its customers depend on?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"ev-1\", \"Morgan Stanley Research, reported by MarketScale, estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\", [\"clean-3\", \"clean-4\"]], [\"ev-2\", \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; as a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5–10% on the GB200 to 25–30% on the VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", [\"clean-3\", \"mem-1\"]], [\"ev-3\", \"NVIDIA's Vera Rubin first shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, according to the item.\", [\"clean-3\"]], [\"ev-4\", \"The item reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\", [\"mem-1\"]], [\"ev-5\", \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the standard estimate, a reminder that procurement structure materially moves the number.\", [\"clean-3\", \"mem-1\"]]]}\n{\"n\": 9, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [[\"clean-1\", \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\"], [\"clean-2\", \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\"], [\"clean-3\", \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\"], [\"clean-4\", \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\"], [\"clean-5\", \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\"], [\"clean-6\", \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"], [\"new-rubin-ultra-kyber-2027\", \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack designed to house its Rubin Ultra chips and carry its high-end data-center value — begin shipping by end of calendar 2027 as originally slated, or slip to 2028 (or later) as SemiAnalysis reports?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"e1-kyber-delay\", \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture — the NVL144 cabinet that packs 144 of Nvidia's most powerful chips and had been slated to debut with the Vera Rubin Ultra system in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis added that NVL576, a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", [\"new-rubin-ultra-kyber-2027\"]], [\"e2-nvidia-denial\", \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber/Rubin Ultra delay, stating that \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", [\"new-rubin-ultra-kyber-2027\"]], [\"e3-backup-plan-cancelled\", \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to reach similar power — was cancelled after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on its odd design and heavy operational burden.\", [\"new-rubin-ultra-kyber-2027\"]], [\"e4-no-proven-scaleup-solution\", \"SemiAnalysis said the cancelled bridge-rack plan leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" and predicted that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google — whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs — a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\", [\"new-rubin-ultra-kyber-2027\"]], [\"e5-rubin-full-production-fall-ship\", \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and will begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to CNBC.\", [\"clean-3\"]], [\"e6-semisanalysis-dc-revenue-estimate\", \"SemiAnalysis projected that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", []]]}\n{\"n\": 10, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\", \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\", \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [[\"clean-1\", \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\"], [\"clean-2\", \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\"], [\"clean-3\", \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\"], [\"clean-4\", \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"], [\"clean-5\", \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"], [\"new-memory-price\", \"Will DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth-memory contract prices stay elevated (or keep rising) through calendar 2026, keeping memory at roughly 25–30% of an NVIDIA AI rack's bill of materials and a potential constraint on AI-system build volumes?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"ev-rack-price\", \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers about $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\", [\"clean-4\"]], [\"ev-memory-content\", \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin (VR200) rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was largely absent before. As a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5–10% on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\", [\"new-memory-price\"]], [\"ev-shipment-schedule\", \"According to the Morgan Stanley-based account in MarketScale, first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\", [\"clean-2\"]], [\"ev-memory-supply\", \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is the force pushing up component costs, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move final rack pricing by $1 million or more per rack.\", [\"new-memory-price\", \"clean-2\"]], [\"ev-self-source-price\", \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million (from about $7.8 million), showing that memory procurement structure materially moves the per-rack number by roughly $1 million or more.\", [\"new-memory-price\"]]]}\n{\"n\": 11, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion — the level Nvidia is said to project.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [[\"clean-1\", \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\"], [\"clean-2\", \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\"], [\"clean-3\", \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\"], [\"clean-4\", \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\"], [\"clean-5\", \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\"], [\"clean-6\", \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"], [\"clean-7\", \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its Rubin Ultra generation, the generation after Vera Rubin — reach first shipments in 2028, after SemiAnalysis reported the program has slipped more than 12 months from a planned 2027 debut?\"], [\"clean-8\", \"How large will NVIDIA's supply/purchase commitments (its obligations to buy components and locked-in capacity) be at its next annual report, and is the ~$119 billion level cited in this item growing faster than the revenue those commitments support?\"], [\"new-1\", \"Will DRAM/memory supply stay tight in calendar 2027 and keep memory chip prices elevated, as Micron expects tight market conditions to last until 2028 and new industry capacity is not due online until 2027 or later?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"ev-1\", \"Micron Technology, in the quarterly update it reported shortly before 7 July 2026, said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, according to Fool.com's Keithen Drury.\", [\"new-1\", \"clean-8\"]], [\"ev-2\", \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury wrote on 7 July 2026 that memory chip prices have skyrocketed because supply has not kept up with massive data center build-out demand, and that new capacity for many in the memory industry will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher.\", [\"new-1\", \"clean-8\"]], [\"ev-3\", \"Micron Technology guided its next quarter (its fiscal Q4 2026, ending August 2026) to about $50 billion in revenue, which Fool.com's Keithen Drury noted is roughly half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\", [\"new-1\"]], [\"ev-4\", \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury stated on 7 July 2026 that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\", [\"clean-6\"]]]}\n{\"n\": 12, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\", \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\", \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [[\"clean-1\", \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\"], [\"clean-2\", \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\"], [\"clean-3\", \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\"], [\"clean-4\", \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"e1\", \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials on the VR200 (from 5-10% on GB200) while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%. Morgan Stanley notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\", [\"clean-4\"]], [\"e2\", \"The article reports that first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, as reported in the context of Morgan Stanley's research.\", [\"clean-3\"]], [\"e3\", \"The article reports that supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory is constrained against AI-driven demand, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, tying memory-market cost pressure to the same supply squeeze behind AI infrastructure costs.\", []]]}\n{\"n\": 13, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [[\"clean-1\", \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\"], [\"clean-2\", \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\"], [\"clean-3\", \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\"], [\"clean-4\", \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"], [\"clean-5\", \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\"], [\"clean-6\", \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"], [\"clean-7\", \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\"], [\"clean-8\", \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\"], [\"clean-9\", \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\"], [\"clean-10\", \"Will the U.S. government (Congress and/or the FCC) enact legislation or regulation to ban or phase out Chinese-made lidar sensors in U.S. autonomous/robotics systems, which would change the sensor options and cost competitiveness available to NVIDIA's DRIVE Hyperion autonomous-vehicle platform?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"ev-1\", \"Micron Technology, in its recent quarterly announcement (reported in this 7 July 2026 article), said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, alongside a quarter of huge growth and strong expectations for the following quarter.\", [\"clean-5\"]], [\"ev-2\", \"fool.com (Keithen Drury) writes that memory prices have skyrocketed because supply has not kept up with data-center-driven demand, and that new industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving inventory low for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher.\", [\"clean-5\"]], [\"ev-3\", \"The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and projects that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which it says will drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\", [\"clean-4\"]], [\"ev-4\", \"The article states that Micron guided next-quarter revenue to $50 billion, which it says is about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate, implying a roughly $100 billion expectation for Nvidia's revenue in that period.\", []]]}\n{\"n\": 14, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [[\"clean-1\", \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\"], [\"clean-2\", \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\"], [\"clean-3\", \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\"], [\"clean-4\", \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"], [\"clean-5\", \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"], [\"clean-6\", \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\"], [\"clean-7\", \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\"], [\"new-mem\", \"Will memory (DRAM/HBM) contract prices stay elevated through calendar 2027 — i.e., will the memory market remain tight as Micron forecasts conditions persisting into 2028 — keeping NVIDIA's memory input costs high and potentially constraining GPU supply?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"ev-1\", \"fool.com's Keithen Drury reports that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026 (this year) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion.\", [\"clean-4\"]], [\"ev-2a\", \"Micron Technology, reporting another strong quarter and strong near-term expectations, said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028.\", [\"new-mem\"]], [\"ev-2b\", \"The article reports that memory chip prices have skyrocketed as supply lags data-center-driven demand, and that new industry capacity won't be online until 2027 or later, keeping supply low for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory prices higher still.\", [\"new-mem\"]], [\"ev-3\", \"Micron Technology guided next quarter to $50 billion in revenue, which the author notes is about half of what NVIDIA is expected to generate.\", [\"new-mem\"]]]}\n{\"n\": 15, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [[\"clean-1\", \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\"], [\"clean-2\", \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\"], [\"clean-3\", \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\"], [\"clean-4\", \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"], [\"clean-5\", \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"], [\"clean-6\", \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\"], [\"clean-7\", \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\"], [\"new-1\", \"What will the average contract price of DRAM (including HBM) memory be in calendar 2027 versus 2026 — will the memory shortage keep DRAM prices rising through 2027 as the item's sources expect?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"ev-1\", \"Micron Technology, in its latest quarterly report before this 2026-07-07 article, said that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, which Fool.com's Keithen Drury describes as huge news alongside strong growth and expectations for the following quarter.\", [\"new-1\"]], [\"ev-2\", \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that new memory capacity for many industry participants will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving memory inventory in low supply for at least the next year and a half, which could push memory chip prices even higher because demand for these devices is not slowing.\", [\"new-1\"]], [\"ev-3\", \"According to Fool.com's Keithen Drury, the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026).\", [\"clean-4\"]], [\"ev-4\", \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that next year (2027) the AI hyperscalers' data center capital expenditures could be over $1 trillion, up from $650 billion planned for 2026; he adds this will lead to even higher demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\", [\"clean-4\"]]]}\n{\"n\": 16, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\"], \"rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [[\"clean-1\", \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\"], [\"clean-2\", \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\"], [\"clean-3\", \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\"], [\"clean-4\", \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"], [\"clean-5\", \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\"], [\"clean-6\", \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"], [\"clean-7\", \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\"], [\"clean-8\", \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"ev-micron-fq3\", \"Micron reported fiscal third-quarter 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%, as cited by columnist Allen Harris.\", [\"clean-5\"]], [\"ev-nvda-margins\", \"Columnist Allen Harris cites Nvidia's latest reported figures in comparing it with Micron: year-over-year revenue growth of about 85%, GAAP gross margin of 74.9% and an operating margin of roughly 66%, adding that Nvidia still earns more dollars than Micron. The item does not specify which quarter these Nvidia figures cover.\", []], [\"ev-goldman-ai-capex\", \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, as cited by columnist Allen Harris.\", [\"clean-4\"]], [\"ev-apple-memory-cost\", \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\", [\"clean-5\"]]]}\n{\"n\": 17, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge. The spending covers chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure required to run AI at scale.\"], \"rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [[\"clean-1\", \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\"], [\"clean-2\", \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\"], [\"clean-3\", \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\"], [\"clean-4\", \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"], [\"clean-5\", \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"], [\"clean-6\", \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\"], [\"clean-7\", \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"e1\", \"According to the article's comparison with Micron, NVIDIA's latest reported quarter showed roughly 85% revenue growth, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9%, and an operating margin of about 66%, and NVIDIA still earns more dollars in absolute terms than Micron despite Micron's faster growth and fatter margins.\", []], [\"e2\", \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, a spending category the article says includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure required to run AI at scale.\", [\"clean-4\"]], [\"e3\", \"Micron, a memory-chip maker whose high-bandwidth memory (HBM) demand the article ties to AI accelerators such as NVIDIA's, reported fiscal Q3 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion, up about 346% from $9.30 billion a year earlier; GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion a year earlier; a gross margin of 84.6% (up from 37.7%) and an operating margin of 80.4% (up from 23.3%).\", []]]}\n{\"n\": 18, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [[\"clean-1\", \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have committed to AI cloud partners for their NVIDIA-powered compute infrastructure, and how large is the resulting guarantee/credit exposure?\"], [\"clean-2\", \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\"], [\"clean-3\", \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI chip platform begin first shipments — the step that brings revenue — before the end of calendar 2026?\"], [\"clean-4\", \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"], [\"clean-5\", \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\"], [\"clean-6\", \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"], [\"clean-7\", \"What will the outstanding amount of NVIDIA's supply (purchase) commitments to suppliers be at its next annual report — the obligations that lock it into buying capacity and services for its AI systems?\"], [\"clean-8\", \"How much revenue will the big four hyperscalers' in-house AI accelerators (Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia, Meta MTIA) generate in calendar 2026 — the combined custom-silicon quantity competing with NVIDIA GPUs, whose customers also fund NVIDIA's data center segment?\"], [\"clean-9\", \"What share of Chinese enterprises' AI accelerator budget will be allocated to domestic (Chinese-made) products versus Nvidia over the 12 months following the July 2026 survey, and will it rise toward the 46% executives indicated from 30% today?\"], [\"clean-10\", \"Will the U.S. government (Congress and/or the FCC) enact legislation or regulation to ban or phase out Chinese-made lidar sensors in U.S. autonomous/robotics systems, which would change the sensor options and cost competitiveness available to NVIDIA's DRIVE Hyperion autonomous-vehicle platform?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"ev-1\", \"Micron Technology announced another quarter of large growth and gave very strong guidance for the following quarter, and said it expects tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, per a Fool.com article by Keithen Drury. If the tightness lasts that long, it implies a longer period of elevated memory supply scarcity affecting buyers such as Nvidia.\", [\"clean-5\"]], [\"ev-2\", \"Micron Technology guided revenue for its next quarter to $50 billion, which the article says is about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\", [\"clean-5\"]], [\"ev-3\", \"The article's author writes that new memory capacity for many industry players will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory supply for at least the next year and a half and potentially driving memory chip prices even higher as demand does not slow.\", [\"clean-5\"]], [\"ev-4\", \"According to the article, AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditure this year, and next year that figure could be over $1 trillion, which the author says will raise demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\", [\"clean-4\", \"clean-5\"]]]}\n{\"n\": 19, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"The four largest AI hyperscalers plan to spend roughly $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the fool.com analysis, which describes this planned spend as the baseline for a sharp increase it expects next year (published 2026-07-07).\", \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), up from around $650 billion planned for 2026, as reported by fool.com (published 2026-07-07).\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [[\"clean-1\", \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\"], [\"clean-2\", \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\"], [\"clean-3\", \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\"], [\"clean-4\", \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"], [\"clean-5\", \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"], [\"clean-6\", \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\"], [\"clean-7\", \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"e1\", \"The article reports that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026 (\\\"this year\\\"). No individual hyperscaler is named as the source of the aggregate figure.\", [\"clean-4\"]], [\"e2\", \"NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), as reported by the fool.com article.\", [\"clean-4\"]]]}\n{\"n\": 20, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [[\"clean-1\", \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\"], [\"clean-2\", \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\"], [\"clean-3\", \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\"], [\"clean-4\", \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\"], [\"clean-5\", \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\"], [\"clean-6\", \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"], [\"new-1\", \"Will Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system that houses its next-generation Vera Rubin Ultra chips and had been slated to debut in 2027 — reach first shipments in calendar 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"e1\", \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported in a post that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet designed to house Nvidia's 2027 Rubin Ultra chips and slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system remains difficult to manufacture. SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", [\"new-1\"]], [\"e2\", \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has slipped to 2028, saying \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", [\"new-1\"]], [\"e3\", \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia scrapped a backup plan to bolt two of its current-generation racks together to match Rubin Ultra's scale, after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on the design as awkward and operationally burdensome; the firm said this leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra\\\" and predicted the gap could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\", [\"new-1\"]], [\"e4\", \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to CNBC.\", [\"clean-3\"]], [\"e5\", \"SemiAnalysis projects that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", []]]}\n{\"n\": 21, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"refs\": [\"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [[\"clean-1\", \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners for NVIDIA-powered capacity, large enough to affect its credit risk, by the 2027-12-31 check date?\"], [\"clean-2\", \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line carrying its data-center value, begin shipping by the end of 2026?\"], [\"clean-3\", \"Will China's government allow NVIDIA to sell its H200 AI chips into mainland China, the world's second-largest chip market, by the check date — i.e. will Beijing lift the block that currently keeps NVIDIA's China share at zero despite U.S. clearance?\"], [\"clean-4\", \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"], [\"clean-5\", \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"], [\"clean-6\", \"How much will NVIDIA's own supply purchase commitments total at its next annual report — the item cites $119 billion in supply commitments, a purchase obligation large enough to bear on its cash and credit risk?\"], [\"clean-7\", \"How much will Amazon, Google, Microsoft and Meta spend in calendar 2026 on custom AI accelerators (such as Trainium, TPU, Maia and MTIA), a measure of how much AI compute demand bypasses NVIDIA's GPUs?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"ev-1\", \"In a fool.com column, contributor Keithen Drury wrote that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures this year (2026), and that next year (2027) that figure could be over $1 trillion; he infers this would drive even higher demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\", [\"clean-4\"]], [\"ev-2\", \"Micron Technology said in announcing its latest quarterly results that memory market conditions are tight and could last until 2028; the fool.com article adds that new memory-industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low inventory supply for at least the next year and a half and possibly pushing memory chip prices even higher as demand does not slow.\", [\"clean-6\"]], [\"ev-3\", \"Micron Technology guided next quarter (the fiscal quarter following the one ending in August 2026) to $50 billion in revenue, a figure the fool.com author describes as about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\", []]]}\n{\"n\": 22, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"K1\", \"claim\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture is delayed (beyond its planned 2027).\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"K2\", \"claim\": \"NVIDIA rejected the delay report, saying its roadmap is intact.\", \"year\": null}], \"refs\": [\"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\"], \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the one thesis that holds the Kyber timing (open or new).\", \"usable\": true, \"theses\": [[\"clean-1\", \"How much credit support will NVIDIA have extended to AI cloud partners under its new revenue-sharing / credit-support model, and does it materially raise NVIDIA's contingent obligations and credit risk?\"], [\"clean-2\", \"What will the CPU-to-GPU attach ratio in AI inference workloads be in 2026, as Intel CEO Lip-Bu Tan says it is beginning to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs toward 1 CPU to 1 GPU?\"], [\"clean-3\", \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin next-generation AI chip platform, the product line that carries its data center value, begin shipping by a check date (end of calendar 2026)?\"], [\"clean-4\", \"How many advanced AI accelerators will Huawei, together with its chipmaking partner SMIC, produce in calendar 2026 (combined Huawei-designed Ascend output: 910B, 910C and the new 950PR line)?\"], [\"clean-5\", \"Will Nvidia actually resume selling H200 AI chips into China by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end — i.e., will Beijing permit Chinese commercial users to buy the chips the U.S. cleared for export in December 2025?\"], [\"clean-6\", \"What will the big four AI hyperscalers' combined data center capital expenditures be in calendar 2027, and will they approach the roughly $1 trillion level Nvidia projects for next year?\"], [\"new-kyber\", \"Will Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture (the NVL144 144-GPU cabinet that was slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra) begin shipping in calendar 2027 as originally scheduled, or slip into 2028?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"ev-1\", \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture, which had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system remains difficult to manufacture. Nvidia rejected the report.\", [\"new-kyber\"]], [\"ev-2\", \"Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture is delayed to 2028, saying \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\", [\"new-kyber\", \"clean-3\"]], [\"ev-3\", \"SemiAnalysis said NVL576 — the larger Nvidia system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", [\"new-kyber\"]], [\"ev-4\", \"A backup plan to bolt two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate, according to SemiAnalysis.\", [\"new-kyber\"]], [\"ev-5\", \"SemiAnalysis said the cancelled workaround leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" and predicted this could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the AI market.\", [\"new-kyber\"]], [\"ev-6\", \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to CNBC.\", [\"clean-3\"]], [\"ev-7\", \"SemiAnalysis projects that Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", []]]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"packet-claims-1.json\\\"));\nfor x in p[11:17]:\n print(json.dumps({\\\"n\\\":x[\\\"n\\\"],\\\"claims\\\":x[\\\"claims\\\"],\\\"usable\\\":x[\\\"answer_usable\\\"],\\\"rule\\\":x[\\\"link_rule\\\"],\\\"theses\\\":[(t[\\\"id\\\"],t[\\\"question\\\"]) for t in x[\\\"open_theses\\\"]+x[\\\"new_theses\\\"] if any(w in t[\\\"question\\\"].lower() for w in [\\\"capex\\\",\\\"capital expenditure\\\",\\\"kyber\\\",\\\"memory\\\"])],\\\"evidence\\\":[(e[\\\"id\\\"],e[\\\"summary\\\"],e[\\\"links\\\"]) for e in x[\\\"kept_evidence\\\"]]},ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"packet-claims-1.json\\\"));\nfor x in p[11:17]:\n print(json.dumps({\\\"n\\\":x[\\\"n\\\"],\\\"claims\\\":x[\\\"claims\\\"],\\\"usable\\\":x[\\\"answer_usable\\\"],\\\"rule\\\":x[\\\"link_rule\\\"],\\\"theses\\\":[(t[\\\"id\\\"],t[\\\"question\\\"]) for t in x[\\\"open_theses\\\"]+x[\\\"new_theses\\\"] if any(w in t[\\\"question\\\"].lower() for w in [\\\"capex\\\",\\\"capital expenditure\\\",\\\"kyber\\\",\\\"memory\\\"])],\\\"evidence\\\":[(e[\\\"id\\\"],e[\\\"summary\\\"],e[\\\"links\\\"]) for e in x[\\\"kept_evidence\\\"]]},ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"{\"n\": 12, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"M1\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M2\", \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\", \"year\": null}, {\"claim_id\": \"M3\", \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\", \"year\": null}], \"usable\": true, \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to at least one thesis.\", \"theses\": [[\"clean-4\", \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"e1\", \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials on the VR200 (from 5-10% on GB200) while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%. Morgan Stanley notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\", [\"clean-4\"]], [\"e2\", \"The article reports that first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, as reported in the context of Morgan Stanley's research.\", [\"clean-3\"]], [\"e3\", \"The article reports that supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory is constrained against AI-driven demand, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, tying memory-market cost pressure to the same supply squeeze behind AI infrastructure costs.\", []]]}\n{\"n\": 13, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"usable\": true, \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"theses\": [[\"clean-4\", \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"], [\"clean-5\", \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\"], [\"clean-6\", \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"ev-1\", \"Micron Technology, in its recent quarterly announcement (reported in this 7 July 2026 article), said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, alongside a quarter of huge growth and strong expectations for the following quarter.\", [\"clean-5\"]], [\"ev-2\", \"fool.com (Keithen Drury) writes that memory prices have skyrocketed because supply has not kept up with data-center-driven demand, and that new industry capacity will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving inventory low for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory chip prices even higher.\", [\"clean-5\"]], [\"ev-3\", \"The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and projects that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which it says will drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\", [\"clean-4\"]], [\"ev-4\", \"The article states that Micron guided next-quarter revenue to $50 billion, which it says is about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate, implying a roughly $100 billion expectation for Nvidia's revenue in that period.\", []]]}\n{\"n\": 14, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"usable\": true, \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"theses\": [[\"clean-4\", \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"], [\"clean-5\", \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"], [\"new-mem\", \"Will memory (DRAM/HBM) contract prices stay elevated through calendar 2027 — i.e., will the memory market remain tight as Micron forecasts conditions persisting into 2028 — keeping NVIDIA's memory input costs high and potentially constraining GPU supply?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"ev-1\", \"fool.com's Keithen Drury reports that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026 (this year) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion.\", [\"clean-4\"]], [\"ev-2a\", \"Micron Technology, reporting another strong quarter and strong near-term expectations, said tight memory market conditions could last until 2028.\", [\"new-mem\"]], [\"ev-2b\", \"The article reports that memory chip prices have skyrocketed as supply lags data-center-driven demand, and that new industry capacity won't be online until 2027 or later, keeping supply low for at least the next year and a half and potentially pushing memory prices higher still.\", [\"new-mem\"]], [\"ev-3\", \"Micron Technology guided next quarter to $50 billion in revenue, which the author notes is about half of what NVIDIA is expected to generate.\", [\"new-mem\"]]]}\n{\"n\": 15, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"claim\": \"About $650 billion of data-center capital expenditures planned by AI hyperscalers for 2026.\", \"year\": \"2026\"}, {\"claim_id\": \"C27\", \"claim\": \"Those expenditures projected to reach or exceed about $1 trillion in 2027.\", \"year\": \"2027\"}], \"usable\": true, \"rule\": \"Each claim must be linked to the capex thesis of its year when that year's thesis is open or newly opened in this answer; otherwise to the open capex thesis.\", \"theses\": [[\"clean-4\", \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"], [\"clean-5\", \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"], [\"new-1\", \"What will the average contract price of DRAM (including HBM) memory be in calendar 2027 versus 2026 — will the memory shortage keep DRAM prices rising through 2027 as the item's sources expect?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"ev-1\", \"Micron Technology, in its latest quarterly report before this 2026-07-07 article, said that tight memory market conditions could last until 2028, which Fool.com's Keithen Drury describes as huge news alongside strong growth and expectations for the following quarter.\", [\"new-1\"]], [\"ev-2\", \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that new memory capacity for many industry participants will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving memory inventory in low supply for at least the next year and a half, which could push memory chip prices even higher because demand for these devices is not slowing.\", [\"new-1\"]], [\"ev-3\", \"According to Fool.com's Keithen Drury, the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026).\", [\"clean-4\"]], [\"ev-4\", \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that next year (2027) the AI hyperscalers' data center capital expenditures could be over $1 trillion, up from $650 billion planned for 2026; he adds this will lead to even higher demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\", [\"clean-4\"]]]}\n{\"n\": 16, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"usable\": true, \"rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"theses\": [[\"clean-4\", \"Will the combined data center capital expenditure of the big four AI hyperscalers reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027, the aggregate AI-infrastructure spend Nvidia projects and on which its GPU demand depends?\"], [\"clean-5\", \"What will contract prices for AI memory (LPDDR5X, HBM, 3D NAND/DRAM) be through calendar 2026-2027 — the input that now makes up roughly 25-30% of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack cost?\"], [\"clean-6\", \"Will NVIDIA's Rubin Ultra rack-scale platform — the Kyber NVL144 architecture that houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs — reach first shipments in 2027 as originally planned, or slip to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"ev-micron-fq3\", \"Micron reported fiscal third-quarter 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion versus $9.30 billion a year earlier (roughly 346% growth), GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion, gross margin of 84.6% versus 37.7%, and operating margin of 80.4% versus 23.3%, as cited by columnist Allen Harris.\", [\"clean-5\"]], [\"ev-nvda-margins\", \"Columnist Allen Harris cites Nvidia's latest reported figures in comparing it with Micron: year-over-year revenue growth of about 85%, GAAP gross margin of 74.9% and an operating margin of roughly 66%, adding that Nvidia still earns more dollars than Micron. The item does not specify which quarter these Nvidia figures cover.\", []], [\"ev-goldman-ai-capex\", \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking, as cited by columnist Allen Harris.\", [\"clean-4\"]], [\"ev-apple-memory-cost\", \"Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs.\", [\"clean-5\"]]]}\n{\"n\": 17, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"G\", \"claim\": \"Goldman Sachs estimates that annual AI-infrastructure capital expenditures could reach about $765 billion in 2026 (and about $1.6 trillion by 2031).\", \"year\": null}], \"usable\": true, \"rule\": \"The claim must be linked to the open capex thesis.\", \"theses\": [[\"clean-4\", \"How much will the big four AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, and does their combined spend approach the ~$1 trillion level NVIDIA projects for 2027?\"], [\"clean-5\", \"Will NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the rack system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — begin shipping by end-2027 as originally scheduled, or has it slipped to 2028 as SemiAnalysis reports?\"]], \"evidence\": [[\"e1\", \"According to the article's comparison with Micron, NVIDIA's latest reported quarter showed roughly 85% revenue growth, a GAAP gross margin of 74.9%, and an operating margin of about 66%, and NVIDIA still earns more dollars in absolute terms than Micron despite Micron's faster growth and fatter margins.\", []], [\"e2\", \"Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, a spending category the article says includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure required to run AI at scale.\", [\"clean-4\"]], [\"e3\", \"Micron, a memory-chip maker whose high-bandwidth memory (HBM) demand the article ties to AI accelerators such as NVIDIA's, reported fiscal Q3 2026 revenue of $41.46 billion, up about 346% from $9.30 billion a year earlier; GAAP net income of $28.24 billion versus $1.89 billion a year earlier; a gross margin of 84.6% (up from 37.7%) and an operating margin of 80.4% (up from 23.3%).\", []]]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"packet-claims-1.json\\\"));\nfor x in p:\n print(x[\\\"n\\\"],x[\\\"answer_usable\\\"],\\\" | \\\".join(v[\\\"summary\\\"] for v in x[\\\"stored_reference_items\\\"]))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"packet-claims-1.json\\\"));\nfor x in p:\n print(x[\\\"n\\\"],x[\\\"answer_usable\\\"],\\\" | \\\".join(v[\\\"summary\\\"] for v in x[\\\"stored_reference_items\\\"]))'\"","aggregated_output":"1 True Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the cabinet designed to house 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, packing 144 GPUs per unit — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane that connects the system's modules remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint. The design had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027. Nvidia rejected the report (see separate item). | Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed, stating that its roadmap is intact.\n2 True Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the cabinet designed to house 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, packing 144 GPUs per unit — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the specialized multi-layer PCB midplane that connects the system's modules remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint. The design had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra in 2027. Nvidia rejected the report (see separate item). | Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report that its Kyber rack-scale architecture has been delayed, stating that its roadmap is intact.\n3 True The four largest AI hyperscalers plan to spend roughly $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the fool.com analysis, which describes this planned spend as the baseline for a sharp increase it expects next year (published 2026-07-07). | NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), up from around $650 billion planned for 2026, as reported by fool.com (published 2026-07-07).\n4 True The four largest AI hyperscalers plan to spend roughly $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the fool.com analysis, which describes this planned spend as the baseline for a sharp increase it expects next year (published 2026-07-07). | NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), up from around $650 billion planned for 2026, as reported by fool.com (published 2026-07-07).\n5 True Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes. | Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\n6 True Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling and networking.\n7 True Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion — the level Nvidia is said to project.\n8 True Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%. | Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number. | MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\n9 True Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes. | Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\n10 True In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%. | Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price. | The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\n11 True Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (2026) and that next year (2027) that figure could exceed $1 trillion — the level Nvidia is said to project.\n12 True Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%. | Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number. | MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\n13 True The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\n14 True Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\n15 True Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\n16 True Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\n17 True Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge. The spending covers chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure required to run AI at scale.\n18 True The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\n19 True The four largest AI hyperscalers plan to spend roughly $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the fool.com analysis, which describes this planned spend as the baseline for a sharp increase it expects next year (published 2026-07-07). | NVIDIA claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capital expenditures next year (calendar 2027), up from around $650 billion planned for 2026, as reported by fool.com (published 2026-07-07).\n20 True Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes. | Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\n21 True Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\n22 True Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the NVL144 rack's multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture; SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes. | Nvidia rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber delay and stated that its product roadmap is intact.\n23 True Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in 2026, and that next year (2027) the figure could exceed $1 trillion, which he says would drive even higher demand for memory chips and Nvidia GPUs.\n24 True The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\n25 True Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\n26 True Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\n27 True Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack architecture, the rack-scale system designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips, has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028 because the multi-layer PCB midplane remains difficult to manufacture, and that the larger NVL576 system (eight racks linked optically) is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes. SemiAnalysis concluded this leaves NVIDIA with no proven solution to expand scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra, which it said could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market. | NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis's report of a Kyber/Rubin Ultra delay, stating that \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\n28 True Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, as cited by Allen Harris in The Berkshire Edge. The spending covers chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure required to run AI at scale.\n29 True Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year, up from the group's planned spend of around $650 billion this year, according to a fool.com article.\n30 True Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion planned this year (2026), as reported by the article. Both are forward projections/plans, not disclosed actual results.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
